This will be the last one which is the direct Delta method approach that doesn't have restrictions on there being equal numbers of visits within each habitat.

If the logit of psi_i is labeled w_i (which is what occu estimates), then the mean of the anti-logits is given by

Then the partial derivative with respect to w_i is

So using the Delta method an estimate of the standard error of the mean of the anti-logits is coded with

# Run analysis
  fm <- occu(~ 1 ~ 0 + habitat, umf)

# Partial derivatives evaluated at the maximum likelihood estimates
  f <- exp(fm@opt$par[1:5])/(1+exp(fm@opt$par[1:5]))^2/5

# Get covariance matrix
  covmat <- solve(fm@opt$hessian)

# Calculate standard error
  psi.mean.se <http://psi.mean.se> <- sqrt(f%*%covmat[1:5, 1:5]%*%f)
  #            [,1]
  # [1,] 0.01286598

Jim

