Dear Jim

Thank you for your detailed answers. I see there is no easy answer to my questions... and that calculating the appropriate standard error of the averaged occupancy may be more complex than I thought. But I am glad to see that the estimates don't differ very much (at least for this setting with relatively high occupancy and detection rates, I can try with lower rates).
Do I interpret correctly that you would rather average over the habitat-specific estimates on the anti-logit (probability) scale in any case?

In your second method you wrote:
psi.mean.se <http://psi.mean.se> <- sqrt(sum(covmat[1:k, 1:k]))/k

which takes the square root over the summed variances and only later divides by k. Is that mathematically correct (perhaps because the variance is the actual unit being estimated)? To me it seems strange to not sum over k values and divide by 5 immediately before doing any other transformation.

Best wishes
Gesa

